# Planning a complete generation

A parsed project describes accepted structure. Generation adds the policies needed
to produce source and documentation: dependency validation, template availability,
output paths, theme selection, expansion limits, destination collisions, source
tangling, and HTML rendering.

The result is a plan, not a write operation. A host receives either every complete
file or diagnostics with an empty file list.

## Why partial output is unsafe

Suppose a project produces three source files. The first two can be expanded, but
the third contains a missing fragment reference. Returning the first two would make
the filesystem represent no coherent project version. Another tool might compile
new versions of two modules against an old third module.

Literate therefore treats output planning as one atomic decision:

```text
files = all planned outputs, if diagnostics is empty
files = no outputs, otherwise
```

The equation is intentionally simple. Atomic planning does not claim that a
filesystem can replace many files at one physical instant. It guarantees that the
host never receives a knowingly partial semantic plan.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Parsed[Parsed project] --> Validate[Validate complete graph and policy]
    Validate --> Source[Expand every source root]
    Validate --> Docs[Render every document]
    Source --> Complete{All outputs complete?}
    Docs --> Complete
    Complete -->|No| Reject[Diagnostics and an empty file list]
    Complete -->|Yes| Plan[Complete generation plan]
```

## Validate before expensive presentation

Generation establishes accepted inputs, roots, and destinations before invoking
renderers that may perform substantial in-memory work. Publication follows an even
stricter version of this pattern by validating the complete document before calling
trusted contribution renderers.

Ordering validation ahead of presentation has two benefits. A late source error
cannot leave a half-rendered result, and callbacks cannot observe a project that
will later be rejected for a known structural reason.

## Source expansion remains literal

Tangling recursively replaces references with their target contributions and
preserves reference-site indentation. Escaped opening delimiters become literal
text exactly once after expansion. Replacement text is not rescanned as a new
template or a second fragment language.

A source template receives the complete code at its `[CODE]` marker. An HTML
template receives rendered content and controlled project metadata at its own
markers. Both substitutions are literal and single-pass, preventing author text
from accidentally becoming another instruction to the template mechanism.

## Reserve every destination

Two roots must not silently write the same path. Source outputs must not collide
with documentation pages, templates, inputs, portable companions, or destinations
that are equivalent on common case-insensitive filesystems.

Path policy is deliberately portable rather than reflecting only the current host.
A project accepted on one operating system should not become ambiguous merely
because it is checked out on another.

The plan records normalized relative destinations and complete content. It does not
open those destinations or infer that an apparently missing path is safe. The host
performs filesystem-specific preflight later.

## Bounds reject rather than truncate

Expansion has limits on nesting, segments, and intermediate text. HTML and
publication paths have their own limits. When a limit is crossed, generation
reports the problem and returns no files.

If `S` is the accepted segment limit and a proposed expansion requires `S + 1`
segments, the result is rejection—not an output containing the first `S` segments.
Truncation would change program meaning while disguising the failure as success.

## Documentation is part of the plan

Generation produces both tangled source and woven HTML. A broken source graph also
prevents replacing the documentation, because the two products describe one
snapshot. This does not mean preview must wait for disk generation: the editor can
render a live captured buffer through a separate screen path.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Plan[Complete generation plan] --> Sources[Tangled program source]
    Plan --> Pages[Woven HTML chapters]
    Plan --> Index[Book index]
    Sources --> Transaction[Host output transaction]
    Pages --> Transaction
    Index --> Transaction
```

## The host owns commitment

Once a complete plan exists, a host may compare it with disk, report differences,
or commit changed outputs. The CLI and editor share a transaction protocol but
supply different filesystem adapters.

Planning before effects creates a reusable architecture:

1. capture explicit inputs;
2. parse and validate;
3. construct every output in memory;
4. return diagnostics or a complete plan;
5. let the host preflight and commit that plan.

The next part follows this architecture into its hosts, beginning with the simplest
adapter and ending with the fixed-point check that lets a generated engine verify
itself.
